\begin{abstract}
Multi-agent LLM systems increasingly tackle complex reasoning tasks through diverse coordination topologies, yet their termination behavior remains poorly understood. Current stopping criteria---maximum turns, keyword matching, or external signals---are applied uniformly regardless of how agents coordinate, leading to premature termination that sacrifices quality or wasteful over-computation. We present the first systematic study of termination dynamics across 13 coordination patterns plus a single-agent baseline spanning six topology categories: single-agent baseline (S), sequential chain (A), centralized routing/star (B1), decentralized handoff/mesh (B2), structured feedback (C), and composed/nested (D) architectures. Through five interconnected experiments totaling over 1,880 runs within a unified AutoGen framework, we characterize how topology fundamentally shapes when teams should stop. Our analysis reveals that (1) centralized routing (B1) and decentralized handoff (B2), previously lumped as ``dynamic routing,'' exhibit fundamentally different cost scaling (sub-linear vs.\ super-linear), (2) a 3-agent decentralized swarm is more efficient than a 2-agent sequential chain---a non-trivial finding, (3) the marginal utility criterion $\Delta U(t) = \Delta Q(t) - \lambda \cdot \Delta C(t) \to 0$ naturally converges via quality saturation, providing a topology-aware optimal stopping signal, (4) the new cost hierarchy $\text{A} \approx \text{B2} < \text{B1} \approx \text{C} \ll \text{D}$ replaces the previous $\text{A} \ll \text{B} \approx \text{C} \ll \text{D}$, and (5) keyword-based termination is a surprisingly effective heuristic that aligns with quality plateau points, and a diagnostic $\Delta U$ experiment reveals where keyword heuristics succeed and where they fail. We release our experimental framework and propose design guidelines for topology-aware termination.
\end{abstract}

%=========================================================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
%=========================================================================

\subsection{The Termination Problem in Multi-Agent Teams}

The deployment of large language model (LLM) based multi-agent systems has expanded rapidly, with frameworks such as AutoGen \citep{wu2023autogen}, CrewAI, and LangGraph enabling diverse coordination patterns for complex reasoning tasks. These systems coordinate multiple specialized agents through topologies ranging from simple round-robin chains to sophisticated debate protocols and hierarchical pipelines. However, a fundamental question remains largely unexplored: \emph{when should these teams stop?}

Current practice relies on rudimentary stopping criteria applied uniformly across topologies: (i)~a fixed maximum number of turns, (ii)~keyword detection (e.g., ``TERMINATE''), and (iii)~external timeout signals. These heuristics ignore a critical insight: \emph{the optimal stopping point depends fundamentally on how agents coordinate}. A round-robin team of two agents solving a factual question may reach a satisfactory answer in two turns, while a three-agent debate team may need several exchange rounds to converge. Applying the same termination logic to both configurations inevitably leads to either premature stopping (quality loss) or unnecessary computation (resource waste).

We term this mismatch \emph{termination regret}---the gap between when a team actually stopped and when it should have stopped to optimize the quality-cost tradeoff.

\subsection{Research Questions}

This work investigates five research questions:

\textbf{RQ1}: How do termination dynamics---duration, turn count, token consumption---differ across coordination topologies? (Experiment~01)

\textbf{RQ2}: Can we detect termination regret by measuring per-turn quality trajectories and identifying optimal stopping points post hoc? (Experiment~02)

\textbf{RQ3}: Do structured feedback patterns exhibit measurable convergence signals that could inform adaptive stopping? (Experiment~03)

\textbf{RQ4}: Do different topologies produce characteristic error patterns? (Experiment~04)

\textbf{RQ5}: Does the marginal utility $\Delta U(t) = \Delta Q(t) - \lambda \cdot \Delta C(t)$ converge to zero at the optimal stopping point, and can this serve as a topology-aware termination signal? (Experiment~05)

\subsection{Contributions}

We make five contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Cross-topology termination study.} The first systematic comparison of termination behavior across 13 coordination patterns within a single unified framework (AutoGen), spanning five topology categories. Prior work has compared at most 2--3 patterns across different frameworks, confounding topology effects with implementation differences \citep{cemri2025why,hu2025when}.

\item \textbf{Termination regret metric.} A post-hoc quality trajectory analysis using G-Eval \citep{liu2023geval} scoring at each agent turn, enabling precise identification of when quality peaked relative to when the team stopped.

\item \textbf{Claim-level convergence detection.} Extending the KS-test approach of \citet{hu2025when} from debate-only to all 13 topologies via sentence-transformer embeddings \citep{reimers2019sentencebert}.

\item \textbf{Pattern-specific error taxonomy.} Demonstrating that error type distributions correlate with topology features: sequential patterns produce context overflow, while decentralized patterns produce turn explosion.

\item \textbf{Marginal utility as a diagnostic framework.} A diagnostic criterion $\Delta U(t) = \Delta Q(t) - \lambda \cdot \Delta C(t) \to 0$ that naturally converges via quality saturation, revealing where existing keyword-based termination is near-optimal and where it fails. The framework exhibits $\lambda$-insensitivity and topology-dependent convergence speeds (1.1--2.4 turns).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Paper Organization}

Section~\ref{sec:background} surveys related work. Section~\ref{sec:methodology} describes our taxonomy and experimental framework. Section~\ref{sec:results} presents results from five experiments. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses implications and limitations. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.

%=========================================================================
\section{Background and Related Work}
\label{sec:background}
%=========================================================================

\subsection{Multi-Agent Coordination Topologies}

Multi-agent LLM systems employ diverse coordination strategies. Grounded in classical MAS topology theory \citep{masterman2025landscape}---which identifies chain, star, and mesh as canonical topologies---we organize 13 patterns into five categories (Figure~\ref{fig:taxonomy}):

\textbf{Sequential Chain (A).} Round-robin patterns cycle agents in fixed order. Each agent sees the full conversation history but cannot influence routing. Patterns: RR-2, RR-3, RR-4.

\textbf{Centralized Routing / Star (B1).} A dedicated coordinator agent analyzes conversation state and centrally decides which specialist to invoke next, corresponding to the hub-and-spoke topology. Patterns: Sel-3, Sel-4.

\textbf{Decentralized Handoff / Mesh (B2).} Without a central coordinator, each agent autonomously decides when and to whom to hand off control via tool calls. No coordination bottleneck exists, but turn explosion risk is significant. Patterns: Swm-3, Swm-4.

\textbf{Structured Feedback (C).} Agents iteratively refine outputs through explicit feedback loops---reflection (generator-critic) or debate (argumentation with moderator). Patterns: Refl-2, Refl-3, Deb-3, Deb-4.

\textbf{Composed/Nested (D).} Multiple stages or layers are composed into pipeline or mixture-of-agents (MoA) architectures. Patterns: Pipe, MoA.

Prior frameworks have implemented subsets: AutoGen \citep{wu2023autogen} supports round-robin, selector, and swarm; DyTopo \citep{lu2026dytopo} enables dynamic topology switching; MoA \citep{li2025moa} introduces layered aggregation. \citet{wang2025rethinking} apply small-world network theory to multi-agent debate. MegaAgent \citep{sun2025megaagent} demonstrates that inter-agent dialogue tokens dominate total cost at scale. G-Designer \citep{zhang2025gdesigner} uses variational graph auto-encoders to dynamically generate task-specific topologies, achieving up to 95.33\% token reduction. The concurrent ``Scaling Agent Systems'' study \citep{xiong2025scaling} evaluates 5 architectures across 180 configurations. AgentDropout \citep{wang2025agentdropout} optimizes communication graph adjacency matrices to eliminate redundant agents. Our study complements these by focusing specifically on \emph{termination dynamics}.

\subsection{Stopping Criteria in LLM Systems}

\textbf{Single-Agent.} REFRAIN \citep{sun2025refrain} addresses ``overthinking'' in CoT reasoning through a stop discriminator combined with UCB multi-armed bandit control, reducing tokens by 20--55\%. Self-consistency \citep{wang2023selfconsistency} implicitly uses convergence detection.

\textbf{Multi-Agent.} \citet{hu2025when} propose adaptive stability detection for debate via KS-test on Beta-Binomial distributions. \citet{ruan2025reaching} introduce Aegean, a formal consensus protocol with incremental quorum detection achieving 1.2--20$\times$ latency reduction. \citet{cemri2025why} identify termination as one of three failure categories. SupervisorAgent \citep{lin2025stop} introduces an LLM-free context filter for runtime supervision, reducing tokens by 29.45\%.

\textbf{Gap.} No prior work systematically studies how termination dynamics vary across a comprehensive set of coordination topologies. Our work bridges this gap by studying 13 patterns across five categories.

\subsection{Quality Evaluation in Multi-Agent Outputs}

We adopt G-Eval \citep{liu2023geval} as our per-turn quality metric with five dimensions (accuracy, completeness, coherence, usefulness, overall) scored on a 1--5 scale. For error detection, we align with MAST categories.

