}
\end{table}

\subsection{Task Suite}

We design 25 tasks across two dimensions: \emph{cognitive type} (4 categories: factual, analytical, creative, technical) and \emph{subject domain} (9 domains: science, CS, history, philosophy, law/politics, gaming, engineering, business, medicine). This dual classification enables domain-specific topology suitability analysis. Full task descriptions are in Appendix~\ref{app:tasks}.

\subsection{Experimental Framework}

All experiments use AutoGen with a custom \texttt{ClaudeCLIChatCompletionClient} wrapping Claude Haiku 4.5 as the base model. To assess cross-model generalizability, we additionally replicate Experiment~01 with GPT-4o-mini on 5 representative patterns (Section~\ref{sec:cross_model}). Metrics per run include: wall-clock duration, turn/agent turn counts, prompt and completion token counts, stop reason, quality score (Exp02), and convergence point (Exp03).

\subsection{Five Experiments}

\textbf{Experiment~01: Pattern Efficiency} (780 runs). All 13 patterns $\times$ 20 tasks $\times$ 3 repeats.

\textbf{Experiment~02: Termination Quality} (100 runs). G-Eval at each turn for 5 patterns $\times$ 20 tasks, yielding 276 turn-level scores.

\textbf{Experiment~03: Convergence Detection} (analysis on 200 runs). Sentence-BERT embeddings on exp01 v2 data for 8 patterns.

\textbf{Experiment~04: Error Attribution} (analysis on 780+200 runs). Structural error classification from exp01/exp02.

\textbf{Experiment~05: Adaptive Termination} (800 runs). 8 patterns $\times$ 25 tasks $\times$ (1 baseline + 3~$\lambda$ values).

%=========================================================================
\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}
%=========================================================================

\subsection{Experiment 01: Pattern Efficiency (RQ1)}
\label{sec:exp01}

We executed 780 runs (13 patterns $\times$ 20 tasks $\times$ 3 repeats) measuring duration, LLM call count, and token consumption.

\subsubsection{Single-Agent Baseline}
\label{sec:solo_baseline}

To quantify coordination overhead, we establish a single-agent baseline (solo) that processes each task with a single LLM call and keyword-based termination. Table~\ref{tab:solo_baseline} compares solo against the most efficient pattern from each multi-agent category.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Single-agent baseline vs.\ best-in-category multi-agent patterns.}
\label{tab:solo_baseline}
\vspace{-2mm}
{\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Pattern} & \textbf{Cat.} & \textbf{Tokens} & \textbf{Dur.(s)} & \textbf{Keyword\%} & \textbf{vs.\ solo} \\
\midrule
solo & S & 1,287 & 18.7 & 100\% & 1.00$\times$ \\
rr2 & A & 4,759 & 52.4 & 91.7\% & 3.70$\times$ \\
swm3 & B2 & 4,196 & 75.2 & 78.3\% & 3.26$\times$ \\
sel3 & B1 & 7,851 & 115.2 & 100\% & 6.10$\times$ \\
refl2 & C & 5,237 & 60.2 & 100\% & 4.07$\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Finding 0: Multi-agent coordination incurs 3.3--6.1$\times$ token overhead over a single agent.} Even the most efficient multi-agent pattern (swm3, 3.26$\times$) consumes more than three times the tokens of a solo agent. This overhead represents the cost of coordination---context accumulation, routing decisions, and inter-agent communication. The baseline anchors all subsequent comparisons: any multi-agent topology must justify its coordination cost through measurable quality or capability gains.

Table~\ref{tab:cross_category} ranks 12 patterns by total token cost (rr4 excluded due to 71.7\% error rate from an infrastructure bug; see Section~\ref{sec:exp04}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-category efficiency comparison (12 multi-agent patterns + solo baseline; rr4 excluded due to 71.7\% error rate), ranked by total tokens.}
\label{tab:cross_category}
\vspace{-2mm}
{\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcrrrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Pattern} & \textbf{Cat.} & \textbf{Agents} & \textbf{Dur.(s)} & \textbf{Tokens} & \textbf{Out/Turn} & \textbf{vs.\ solo} \\
\midrule
solo & S & 1 & 18.7 & 1,287 & -- & 1.00$\times$ \\
swm3 & B2 & 3 & 75.2 & 4,196 & 337 & 3.26$\times$ \\
rr2 & A & 2 & 52.4 & 4,759 & 1,583 & 3.70$\times$ \\
refl2 & C & 2 & 60.2 & 5,237 & 1,732 & 4.07$\times$ \\
sel3 & B1 & 3+1 & 115.2 & 7,851 & 2,114 & 6.10$\times$ \\
deb3 & C & 3+1 & 156.6 & 9,313 & 1,691 & 7.23$\times$ \\
rr3 & A & 3 & 96.6 & 10,121 & 1,701 & 7.86$\times$ \\
sel4 & B1 & 4+1 & 161.9 & 11,595 & 2,616 & 9.01$\times$ \\
deb4 & C & 4+1 & 180.9 & 11,637 & 1,448 & 9.04$\times$ \\
refl3 & C & 3 & 120.2 & 12,095 & 2,537 & 9.40$\times$ \\
swm4 & B2 & 4 & 185.0 & 13,321 & 330 & 10.35$\times$ \\
pipe & D & 5 & 129.6 & 13,856 & 2,325 & 10.76$\times$ \\
moa & D & 4 & 103.0 & 22,333 & 2,494 & 17.35$\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\vspace{-1mm}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Category A: Flat Sequential.} Adding agents approximately doubles duration (rr3/rr2=1.84$\times$) and total tokens (2.13$\times$). The cost driver is \emph{input token accumulation}: input tokens grow at 2.59$\times$ (versus 1.75$\times$ in LLM calls), confirming superlinear context growth as each agent sees the full conversation history. Error rates scale superlinearly: rr2=8.3\%, rr3=23.3\%, rr4=71.7\%. Only factual tasks maintain 0\% error rate across all patterns. Detailed per-category tables are in Appendix~\ref{app:results} (Tables~\ref{tab:catA}--\ref{tab:catA_full}).

\paragraph{Category B: Dynamic Routing.} Selector and swarm represent two fundamentally different routing strategies. Selector produces few turns (3.5--4.1) with long monologues (2,114--2,616 out tokens/turn), while swarm produces many turns (10.2--25.0) with short handoffs (330--337 out tokens/turn). Critically, their scaling behaviors are opposite: selector is \textbf{sub-linear} (sel4/sel3=1.48$\times$) while swarm is \textbf{super-linear} (swm4/swm3=3.17$\times$). At 3 agents, swm3 is 47\% cheaper than sel3; at 4 agents, swm4 is 15\% more expensive than sel4. This crossover has practical implications: swarm routing should be preferred for small teams ($n \leq 3$), while selector scales more gracefully for larger teams.

\paragraph{Finding: swm3 achieves best overall token efficiency.} Remarkably, swm3 (3-agent swarm, 4,196 tokens) is cheaper than rr2 (2-agent round-robin, 4,759 tokens)---a 12\% cost reduction with 50\% more agents. Intelligent routing compensates for the overhead of additional agents by avoiding full-context broadcasting.

\paragraph{Category C: Structured Feedback.} Reflection scaling is super-linear (refl3/refl2=2.31$\times$), as each additional layer amplifies cumulative context. Debate scaling is sub-linear (deb4/deb3=1.25$\times$), as the structured format constrains per-agent verbosity. Refl-2 (5,237 tokens) is only 10\% more expensive than rr2 while including quality assurance via a critic-approval loop. Output per agent turn \emph{decreases} with team size in debate (deb3=1,691, deb4=1,448), contrasting with the collaborative amplification in Category~A.

\paragraph{Category D: Composed Patterns.} MoA is the most expensive pattern (22,333 tokens, 5.3$\times$ swm3) due to three parallel proposers each generating complete responses plus aggregation. Despite higher cost, MoA is 21\% faster than pipe in wall-clock time (103.0s vs.\ 129.6s) from parallelism. Both composed patterns produce the highest output density per agent turn (pipe: 2,325; MoA: 2,494). Task complexity amplifies costs exponentially: MoA's tech/fact ratio is 2.51$\times$, the highest of any pattern.

\paragraph{Cross-Category Statistical Tests.} Kruskal-Wallis tests on 718 successful runs confirm topology category is a significant predictor of efficiency for all five metrics ($p < 0.001$). Pairwise Mann-Whitney U tests (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:pairwise}) reveal the new cost hierarchy: $\text{A} \approx \text{B2}$ ($p$=0.857) $< \text{B1} \approx \text{C}$ ($p$=0.695) $\ll \text{D}$ ($p < 0.001$). Within-category agent count effects show topology-dependent scaling: A=$\rho$=0.948 (deterministic), B1=$\rho$=0.377 (absorbed), B2=$\rho$=0.704 (explosive), C=$\rho$=0.805 (linear).

\paragraph{Domain Analysis.} Evaluating 8 patterns across 25 tasks spanning 9 domains (Table~\ref{tab:domain_sensitivity} in Appendix), debate3 is uniquely domain-invariant (CV=0.14), while swm3 is domain-sensitive (CV=0.67). Domain cost varies 1.88$\times$ independently of pattern choice (history=7,063 to medicine=13,288 tokens).

