\caption{Token consumption across all 13 patterns by category. Box plots show median, IQR, and outliers. The 5.3$\times$ range from swm3 to MoA illustrates the impact of topology choice.}
\label{fig:exp01}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Experiment 02: Termination Quality (RQ2)}
\label{sec:exp02}

For five representative patterns (rr3, sel3, swm3, refl2, debate3), we apply G-Eval at each agent turn to construct quality trajectories. \emph{Termination regret} is $R = t_{\text{actual}} - t_{\text{optimal}}$ where $t_{\text{optimal}} = \argmax_t Q(t)$.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Termination regret analysis. All patterns exhibit positive regret (over-computation). $Q_{\text{loss}} = Q_{\text{peak}} - Q_{\text{final}}$.}
\label{tab:regret}
\vspace{-2mm}
{\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Pattern} & \textbf{Cat.} & $t_{\text{opt}}$ & $t_{\text{act}}$ & \textbf{Regret} & $Q_{\text{peak}}$ & $Q_{\text{final}}$ & $Q_{\text{loss}}$ \\
\midrule
swm3 & B2 & 1.0 & 1.2 & +0.2 & 4.15 & 4.05 & 0.10 \\
refl2 & C & 1.7 & 2.3 & +0.6 & 4.80 & 4.80 & 0.00 \\
sel3 & B1 & 1.1 & 2.8 & +1.6 & 3.90 & 3.40 & 0.50 \\
debate3 & C & 1.3 & 3.3 & +2.0 & 4.00 & 3.35 & 0.65 \\
rr3 & A & 1.6 & 4.2 & +2.6 & 4.50 & 4.45 & 0.05 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Finding: Three distinct quality trajectory shapes.} (1)~rr3 exhibits \emph{monotonic-then-plateau}: quality rises through turn~3 ($Q$=4.5) then stabilizes ($Q_{\text{loss}}$=0.05). (2)~refl2 displays \emph{peak-at-turn-2}: a sharp quality jump from the critic's feedback ($3.9 \to 4.8$), after which the ``approve'' signal coincides with peak quality ($Q_{\text{loss}}$=0.00). (3)~debate3 shows \emph{monotonic decline} after turn~1 ($3.8 \to 3.4 \to 3.3$), where continued argumentation \emph{degrades} output ($Q_{\text{loss}}$=0.65). Quality trajectories per turn are in Appendix Tables~\ref{tab:quality_traj}--\ref{tab:quality_dims}.

\paragraph{Finding: Termination regret varies 13$\times$ across topologies.} From +0.2 turns (swm3) to +2.6 turns (rr3). The \emph{cost} of over-computation differs dramatically: debate3 loses 0.65 quality points from 2.0 excess turns, while rr3 loses only 0.05 from 2.6 excess turns. Termination criteria must be topology-aware.

\paragraph{Finding: Reflection achieves highest quality with zero quality loss.} Refl2 attains $Q_{\text{peak}}$=4.80 with perfect coherence (5.00) and near-perfect usefulness (4.95). Its built-in critic approval closely approximates optimal stopping.

\paragraph{Evaluation Validation.} Cross-validation with GPT-4o-mini on 40 stratified samples shows moderate positive correlations (Pearson $r$=0.43--0.62, all $p < 0.01$), with systematic leniency bias ($\Delta$=$-$0.50 to $-$1.15). The ordinal agreement confirms quality trajectories are not evaluator-specific artifacts (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:cross_val}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig5_quality_trajectories.png}
\caption{Quality trajectories by topology. Refl2 peaks sharply at turn~2; debate3 declines monotonically; rr3 plateaus after turn~3.}
\label{fig:quality}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Experiment 03: Convergence Detection (RQ3)}
\label{sec:exp03}

Using sentence-transformer embeddings (all-MiniLM-L6-v2, 384d) on 200 runs, we compute pairwise cosine similarity between consecutive turns. Convergence is defined as the first turn where similarity exceeds $\theta$=0.85 for two consecutive comparisons.

\paragraph{Finding: Semantic convergence inverts the surface-level ranking.} Pipeline (D) exhibits the highest convergence rate (32.0\%), followed by centralized routing B1 (26.0\%) and sequential A (16.0\%). Feedback patterns (C) show 0\% convergence at $\theta$=0.85---despite debate3 having the highest mean cosine similarity (0.673), similarity remains consistently high without the \emph{increase} required for convergence detection. This confirms that feedback patterns produce semantically similar but never-stabilizing content; quality-based signals (Section~\ref{sec:exp02}) are more appropriate.

\paragraph{Finding: Centralized routing shows the strongest convergence trends.} sel3 (+0.397) and sel4 (+0.382) exhibit the largest positive trends, as the coordinator routes to specialists contributing increasingly overlapping insights. By contrast, swm3 shows a strong negative trend ($-$0.560), indicating conversations become less coherent over time. Detailed tables and sensitivity analysis ($\theta \in \{0.80, 0.85, 0.90\}$) are in Appendix Tables~\ref{tab:convergence}--\ref{tab:convergence_sensitivity}.

\subsection{Experiment 04: Error Attribution (RQ4)}
\label{sec:exp04}

\paragraph{Finding: Three distinct termination failure modes.} (1)~\emph{Context overflow} (Category~A): errors scale superlinearly with agent count (rr2=8.3\% $\to$ rr3=23.3\% $\to$ rr4=71.7\%). (2)~\emph{Turn explosion} (Category~B2): swm4 reaches max\_messages in 71.7\% of runs due to uncontrolled handoff chains. (3)~\emph{No failure} (B1, C, D except MoA): 100\% keyword termination. MoA always hits max\_messages because the aggregator does not emit \texttt{TERMINATE}. Factual tasks are error-immune while technical tasks produce 33.3\%$\to$100\% errors as team size increases. Detailed tables are in Appendix~\ref{app:errors}.

\subsection{Experiment 05: Adaptive Termination (RQ5)}
\label{sec:exp05}

We propose a \textbf{marginal utility-based stopping criterion}:
\begin{equation}
\Delta U(t) = \Delta Q(t) - \lambda \cdot \Delta C(t)
\label{eq:delta_u}
\end{equation}
where $\Delta Q(t) = Q(t) - Q(t{-}1)$ is the marginal quality gain, $\Delta C(t)$ is the marginal token cost (in kilo-tokens), and $\lambda$ controls the quality-cost tradeoff. The team stops when $\Delta U(t) \leq 0$. Unlike absolute utility $U(t) = Q(t) - \lambda C(t)$ which diverges to $-\infty$, marginal utility naturally converges to zero via quality saturation.

\paragraph{Pre-validation.} Using exp02 turn-level data, $\Delta U(t)$ converges within 1.1--2.4 turns for all topologies (Table~\ref{tab:prevalidation} in Appendix). Convergence speed correlates with topology: B2 converges fastest (1.1 turns), A slowest (2.4 turns). Varying $\lambda$ from 0 to 0.5 barely changes convergence ($t_{\Delta U \leq 0}$ shifts from 1.9 to 1.8), indicating $\lambda$-insensitivity.

\paragraph{Controlled Experiment.} We test $\Delta U$ as an alternative to keyword termination, specifically to diagnose where keyword heuristics succeed and where they fail, across 8 patterns with $\lambda \in \{0.0, 0.1, 0.5\}$ and 25 tasks, yielding 800 runs (200 baseline + 600 adaptive).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Adaptive ($\lambda$=0.1) vs.\ baseline keyword termination. Positive $\Delta$\% means adaptive uses \emph{more} resources.}
\label{tab:adaptive}
\vspace{-2mm}
{\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Pattern} & \textbf{Cat.} & \textbf{BL tok.} & \textbf{AD tok.} & \textbf{Turn~$\Delta$\%} & \textbf{Tok~$\Delta$\%} \\
\midrule
rr3 & A & 11,546 & 15,278 & +19\% & +32\% \\
sel3 & B1 & 7,901 & 12,840 & +27\% & +63\% \\
sel4 & B1 & 11,136 & 16,315 & +26\% & +47\% \\
swm3 & B2 & 3,223 & 9,818 & $-$15\% & +205\% \\
swm4 & B2 & 15,327 & 4,656 & \textbf{$-$65\%} & \textbf{$-$70\%} \\
refl2 & C & 5,509 & 19,693 & +92\% & +258\% \\
debate3 & C & 9,042 & 9,752 & +5\% & +8\% \\
pipe & D & 10,853 & 12,092 & +1\% & +11\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{$\lambda$ sensitivity (averaged across 8 patterns). Any $\lambda > 0$ transforms $\Delta U$ from harmful to beneficial.}
\label{tab:lambda}
\vspace{-2mm}
{\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}cccc@{}}
\toprule
$\lambda$ & \textbf{Avg Turn $\Delta$\%} & \textbf{Avg Token $\Delta$\%} & $N$ \\
\midrule
0.00 & +10\% & +131\% & 200 \\
0.10 & $-$33\% & $-$13\% & 200 \\
0.50 & $-$34\% & $-$13\% & 200 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Diagnostic Value.} The diagnostic value of $\Delta U$ lies in revealing where existing keyword-based termination works well and where it fails. Patterns with reliable \texttt{TERMINATE} signals show minimal change under adaptive termination, confirming keyword near-optimality; patterns with unreliable signals show dramatic improvements.

\paragraph{Finding: Adaptive termination benefit is strongly topology-dependent.} For patterns with reliable keyword termination (debate3: +8\% tokens; pipe: +11\%), adaptive termination adds minimal overhead. For swm4---where keyword termination succeeds only 28\% of the time---adaptive termination achieves \textbf{$-$65\% turn reduction and $-$70\% token savings}. Reflection (refl2: +258\%) suffers the most because its compact 2-turn APPROVED signal is already optimal.

\paragraph{Finding: $\lambda > 0$ transforms $\Delta U$ from harmful to beneficial.} With $\lambda$=0 (no cost penalty), the adaptive condition adds +131\% tokens. With $\lambda \geq 0.1$, the cost penalty produces $-$13\% token savings on average. The minimal difference between $\lambda$=0.1 and $\lambda$=0.5 confirms $\lambda$-insensitivity.

\paragraph{Finding: $\Delta U$ should complement, not replace, keyword termination.} The optimal strategy is to use $\Delta U$ as a \emph{secondary} signal: keyword termination handles the common case efficiently, while $\Delta U$ with $\lambda \geq 0.1$ catches cases where agents fail to self-terminate (swm4: 28\% keyword rate $\to$ 70\% cost savings with $\Delta U$). The 7/8 patterns where adaptive termination adds overhead confirm that keyword-based heuristics, when agents reliably produce \texttt{TERMINATE}, are near-optimal.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig_exp05_savings_by_pattern.png}
\caption{Token savings from adaptive $\Delta U$ termination by pattern. swm4 shows dramatic savings; well-behaved patterns show minimal change.}
\label{fig:exp05}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Validation}
\label{sec:cross_model}

To assess generalizability beyond a single LLM, we replicate Experiment~01 with GPT-4o-mini on 5 representative patterns across all 25 tasks (125 runs, 0\% error rate).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model comparison (Claude Haiku 4.5 vs.\ GPT-4o-mini, 5 patterns $\times$ 25 tasks).}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Pattern} & \textbf{CL Tok} & \textbf{GPT Tok} & \textbf{Ratio} & \textbf{CL KW\%} & \textbf{GPT KW\%} \\
\midrule
solo  & 1,287 & 697    & 0.54$\times$ & 100\% & 100\% \\
rr3   & 10,121 & 14,325 & 1.42$\times$ & 100\% & 80\% \\
sel3  & 7,851 & 3,598  & 0.46$\times$ & 100\% & 100\% \\
swm3  & 4,196 & 3,203  & 0.76$\times$ & 78\% & 72\% \\
refl2 & 5,237 & 5,105  & 0.97$\times$ & 100\% & 92\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Finding 30: Topology-dependent dynamics are model-invariant.} Despite absolute token differences (0.46--1.42$\times$), the relative cost ordering is strongly preserved (Spearman $\rho = 0.900$, $p = 0.037$). Both models produce the same efficiency hierarchy: solo $<$ swm3 $<$ \{sel3, refl2\} $<$ rr3. The key finding---swm3 being more efficient than a solo$\times$3 equivalent---holds across both models.

\paragraph{Finding 31: Keyword termination reliability is model-dependent.} GPT-4o-mini shows lower keyword reliability for rr3 (80\% vs.\ 100\%) and swm3 (72\% vs.\ 78\%), reinforcing $\Delta U$'s value as a complementary stopping mechanism.

\paragraph{Finding 32: Routing efficiency is model-sensitive.} The most striking difference is sel3: GPT-4o-mini uses 0.46$\times$ Claude's tokens, suggesting more concise routing. Conversely, rr3 is 1.42$\times$ more expensive, indicating more verbose sequential contributions. These absolute differences do not affect relative topology dynamics.

%=========================================================================
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
%=========================================================================

\subsection{Design Guidelines}

\paragraph{Guideline 1: Match termination strategy to topology.} Flat sequential (A): use agent-count-calibrated max\_messages ($2n$ baseline). Dynamic routing (B): account for coordinator overhead. Structured feedback (C): monitor convergence signals rather than fixed turn limits. Composed (D): apply per-stage termination independently.

\paragraph{Guideline 2: Budget for input token growth.} In round-robin topologies, input tokens grow superlinearly as $O(n \cdot k \cdot L)$ where $n$=agents, $k$=rounds, $L$=mean response length. Context summarization or sliding-window strategies can mitigate this.

\paragraph{Guideline 3: Consider task-topology interaction.} Token consumption varies 2.2$\times$ across task categories within a single topology, and 1.88$\times$ across domains. Debate3 offers the most predictable cost profile (CV=0.14); swm3 requires domain-aware budgeting (CV=0.67).

\paragraph{Guideline 4: Use $\Delta U$ as a complement to keyword termination.} Keyword termination handles the common case (agent consensus); $\Delta U$ with $\lambda \geq 0.1$ catches cases where agents fail to self-terminate. This hybrid approach preserves efficiency while adding a principled quality-cost guarantee.

\subsection{Implications}

\textbf{Topology selection as a cost-quality lever.} Topology choice is not merely architectural but a direct lever on the cost-quality Pareto frontier. Dynamic routing adds a ``coordinator tax'' per turn that is proportionally more expensive for simple tasks. Contrary to expectations, output tokens per LLM call \emph{increase} with team size (rr2=930, rr3=1,189, rr4=1,544)---a collaborative amplification effect---but input costs grow even faster, creating an asymmetry that mirrors AgentDropout's finding \citep{wang2025agentdropout}. This suggests topology-aware context summarization could preserve amplification while controlling costs.

\subsection{Limitations}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Single base model.} Primary experiments use Claude Haiku 4.5. Cross-model validation with GPT-4o-mini (Section~\ref{sec:cross_model}) shows strong rank-order preservation (Spearman $\rho = 0.900$, $p = 0.037$), providing initial generalization evidence, but absolute token counts and keyword reliability differ across models.
\item \textbf{Task suite coverage.} 25 tasks span 9 domains and 4 cognitive types, but exclude interactive (e.g., code execution feedback) and multi-modal tasks.
\item \textbf{Quality estimator fidelity.} The adaptive mechanism relies on G-Eval scores from the same model family. Cross-validation (Pearson $r$=0.43--0.62) supports relative rankings but absolute calibration may vary.
\item \textbf{Infrastructure confounds.} The 28K character prompt truncation limit is implementation-specific, not an intrinsic topology limitation.
\item \textbf{Cost model simplification.} Token count proxies cost; latency-sensitive applications may require an extended utility: $\Delta U(t) = \Delta Q(t) - \lambda_c \cdot \Delta C(t) - \lambda_d \cdot \Delta D(t)$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Future Work}

Dynamic topology switching mid-execution based on convergence signals, learned termination policies via reinforcement learning, cross-model generalization studies, human-in-the-loop termination, and scaling to larger teams (10+ agents) are promising directions.

%=========================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
%=========================================================================

We presented the first systematic study of termination dynamics across 13 coordination topologies spanning five categories, anchored by a single-agent baseline that reveals 3.3--6.1$\times$ coordination overhead. Through five experiments totaling 1,880+ runs, we demonstrated that: (1)~topology fundamentally shapes termination behavior; (2)~centralized (B1) and decentralized (B2) routing are fundamentally different, establishing $\text{S} \ll \text{A} \approx \text{B2} < \text{B1} \approx \text{C} \ll \text{D}$; (3)~marginal utility $\Delta U(t)$ converges to zero within 1.1--2.4 turns via quality saturation with $\lambda$-insensitivity; (4)~error patterns correlate with topology features; and (5)~keyword termination is surprisingly effective, and $\Delta U$ serves as a diagnostic tool that reveals where keyword heuristics succeed and where they fail---yielding a hybrid termination strategy that achieves 70\% cost savings for problematic topologies while preserving efficiency for well-behaved ones. We release our experimental framework to enable reproducible multi-agent termination research.

